WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.520
- Good grammar isn't just
a way to sound professional

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.740
or feel morally superior in
all of those Facebook debates.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.750
The best way to feel superior
is to not participate

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:09.070
in Facebook debates at all.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:11.427
But as American writer,
Robert Brault once quipped,

00:00:11.427 --> 00:00:13.417
"Do not be surprised when those who ignore

00:00:13.417 --> 00:00:15.437
"the rules of grammar,
also ignore the law.

00:00:15.437 --> 00:00:18.520
"After all the law is
just so much grammar."

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.750
So, in answer to the
question who gives a (beep)

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.340
about the Oxford comma?

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.820
Well, I do.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.670
And, so do most lawyers.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.920
Matters of grammar, syntax and
yes even a misplaced comma,

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.130
have changed the course of history.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.470
On more than one occasion,
a poorly placed comma

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.280
has cost the US government and businesses

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.410
millions of dollars.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.130
Other times, comma placement

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.320
has literally cost people their lives.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.350
Such was the case of Sir Roger Casement,

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.770
an Irish separatist convicted
of treason in Great Britain

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:45.650
during World War I,

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.927
who famously is said to have been quote,

00:00:47.927 --> 00:00:49.470
"hanged on a comma."

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.030
But, more on that later.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.270
Now I know what you're thinking.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:55.060
Isn't this video just like
a half as interesting video?

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:58.100
To which I say no, it's like
five half as interesting videos

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.090
crammed together.

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:00.187
Sue me.

00:01:00.187 --> 00:01:02.770
(upbeat music)

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.510
- [Voiceover] Brought to you by audible.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:07.760
- Let's start off with the
case of the $2 million comma.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.210
Few days inspire more
anger and frustration

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:13.274
here in the United States
than April 15th, Tax Day.

00:01:13.274 --> 00:01:17.000
(suspenseful music)

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.320
But federal income taxes
as we know them today

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.710
have only existed since
the early 20th century,

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:24.160
following the ratification
of the 16th Amendment.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.600
Before that the US government
funded its operations

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:27.540
a bit differently.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.760
And on July 4th, 1789,

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.270
President George Washington
signed the Tariff Act of 1789,

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.170
stipulating that duties would
be laid on quote, "goods,

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.247
wares and merchandise" in order to quote,

00:01:38.247 --> 00:01:39.550
"support the government."

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:40.770
Instead of taxing income,

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.620
the government taxed imported
goods to fund its priorities.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.680
Now, you may know these as tariffs.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.853
And contrary to what president Trump says.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:49.870
Hm, sorry.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:51.730
Former president Trump.

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:53.730
Man that's, it feels kind of good.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.380
Now, where was I?

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:56.290
Oh yes.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:59.170
Contrary to what former
president Trump would say,

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:02.830
Tariffs are not paid by
foreign companies or Chi-nah.

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:05.070
Tariffs are taxes on
imported foreign goods

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.640
paid by Americans.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.250
And for over a century after
our country's founding,

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.960
these foreign import tariffs
served as the primary source

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.060
of the US government's revenue,

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.670
at times accounting for as much as 95%

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:16.650
of the federal budget.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.370
And for an eight year
period beginning in 1862,

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:21.510
the US government briefly
levied income taxes

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.950
as part of a patriotic war effort to

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:26.170
support the Union effort
against the Confederacy.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.580
But after the civil war, the
US returned to using tariffs

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.750
to fund the majority of
government operations.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.220
So, enter president Ulysses S. grant

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.070
who signed the Tariff Act of 1872,

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:38.060
which sought to ease the
burden on the economy

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.450
by exempting a variety
of goods from tariffs.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.380
And that brings us to how one typo

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.390
cost the US government $2 million,

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.890
or approximately, $42
million in today's dollars.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:50.210
And, quick aside, since
we're talking about

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:52.470
typos and commas, Grant was actually born

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:54.120
Hiram Ulysses grant,

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.830
but he owes his name
Ulysses S grant to a typo,

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:58.550
when a Congressman nominated grant

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:00.810
to become a candidate for
West Point Military Academy,

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.260
due to a typo he accidentally
recommended Ulysses S. grant.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.040
And when Grant got to West point,

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.770
he registered as Ulysses Hiram Grant,

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.880
likely due to concern of being made fun of

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:13.600
for having his initials spelled H.U.G.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.220
Yeah, the guy likes a hug.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.710
But eventually he started
going by Ulysses S. Grant.

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:19.590
Like I said, typos can literally change

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:20.860
the course of history.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.050
But, back to what you all came here for,

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:25.640
Hardcore Typographical Syntax,

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.200
and a history lesson on US tariff laws.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.890
Now, all prior tariff acts
had included a list of goods

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.410
that were generally
exempt from being taxed

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.190
upon importation to United States.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:36.747
Among these exempt items were quote,

00:03:36.747 --> 00:03:39.007
"fruit plants, tropical and semi-tropical

00:03:39.007 --> 00:03:41.400
"for the purpose of
propagation or cultivation."

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.230
Notice fruit plants refers to the plants

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:44.370
not the fruit itself.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.380
Buying fruit used to be quite expensive.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.060
The 1870 Tariff Act levied a duty of 20%

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.380
on oranges, lemons,
pineapples, and grapes,

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.620
and a duty of 10% on limes, bananas,

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.100
and essentially all other fruit.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.790
Fruit was a very popular import item,

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:58.300
and thus fruit tariffs represented

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.750
a not insubstantial portion
of the federal budget.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.670
And in the 1872 Tariff Act revision,

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.770
There was a rather unfortunate
and costly drafting error.

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:07.960
The list of exemptions
in the 1872 Tariff Act

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.320
was supposed to read fruit-plants,

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.860
with a hyphen in between,

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:15.190
but instead the Statute red fruit, plants.

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:17.190
So with fruits, plants

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.360
replacing fruit - plants.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.650
Again, I don't know
how we can cover such a

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:23.500
sexy, sexy topic as this.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:24.660
- I do what I want.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.320
- The importers argue that
the Act of 1872 now allowed

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.410
free entry of all tropical
and semi-tropical fruits,

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:32.680
filing refund claims
from the US government

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:35.130
immediately upon arrival
of fruit shipments.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.200
The Secretary of the Treasury
initially rejected this claim

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.550
arguing that the grammatical error

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:41.060
was clearly intended to read otherwise,

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.460
importers, hoping to take
advantage of this loophole,

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:44.930
filed a series of lawsuits,

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:46.950
and once it was clear that
the Treasury Department's

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:48.900
arguments would not hold up in court,

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.020
the treasury relented.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:53.640
And in December 1874, two years
after the unfortunate typo,

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.500
the US government agreed that
imported fruits were tax-free,

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.080
under the 1872 Act,

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.437
and all taxes collected on
imported fruit was refunded,

00:05:01.437 --> 00:05:02.760
$2 million worth.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.647
That typo would end up
costing the US government

00:05:04.647 --> 00:05:07.810
$2 million in revenue which
was approximately $42 million

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:09.120
in 2021 dollars.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.500
And that represented 1.3%
of the entire government's

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:13.970
total tariff income,

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.810
and 0.65% of the entire federal budget.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:19.950
Needless to say, this case was bananas.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:21.670
Now, there are certain
topics that you just

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.000
simply don't talk about in polite company.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.930
Religion, politics, money.

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:28.260
But not even these
topics are as polarizing

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.830
as the Oxford comma,
which ended up costing

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.530
one Maine dairy company $5 million.

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:36.700
And if you see someone who
is not using an Oxford comma,

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:38.410
please call 911.

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:41.310
That monster has to be
reported to the authorities.

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:43.710
So, what is an Oxford comma?

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.240
Well, in a list of three or more items,

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.720
such as lions, tigers, and bears,

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:48.553
Oh my.

00:05:48.553 --> 00:05:50.430
Those who ascribed to
the Oxford comma rule

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.430
would place a comma after tigers,

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:55.440
while others would simply
write lions, tigers and bears,

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:56.670
without the second comma.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.130
And apparently the internet
is very, very divided

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:00.410
on the Oxford comma.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.600
Half of the internet likes to be wrong.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.810
- What would you do with
the brain if you had one?

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:07.820
- So, why does the Oxford comma matter?

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:09.980
Well, the presence or
absence of the Oxford comma

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:11.950
can completely change the
meaning of a sentence.

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:13.237
For example, if I say, "For my birthday,

00:06:13.237 --> 00:06:14.617
"I went out to dinner with my parents,

00:06:14.617 --> 00:06:16.600
"Bill Clinton, and George W. Bush."

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:17.980
That means I had a riveting night out

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:20.210
with my mom, my dad, and
two former presidents.

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:22.347
However, if I say, "I went
out to dinner with my parents

00:06:22.347 --> 00:06:24.430
"Bill Clinton and George W. Bush."

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:28.080
That just raises all kinds
of uncomfortable questions.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.700
Like, why weren't my two
presidential dads around

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:31.830
for me more growing up.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:33.700
I guess they had a
country to run but still,

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:36.020
they could've come to at least
one of my baseball games,

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:38.680
and give me a ride to school
in the Marine One Helicopter.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:40.780
I'll tell you, the
holidays were no picnic.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:43.000
They were extremely divided

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.620
I guess I have some things to work out

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:45.770
with my imaginary parents.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:48.230
Anyway, so here's how
a missing Oxford comma

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:51.900
cost the Oakhurst Dairy in
Portland Maine $5 million.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.730
In 2014 truck drivers for the
Maine Milk and Cream Company

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:56.600
filed a $10 million class-action lawsuit

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.570
alleging Oakhurst had failed
to pay them owed overtime.

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:02.220
At the time, main law required
time and a half payment

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:03.870
for each overtime hour.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.930
But it carved out overtime exceptions

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:07.610
for those who perform
the following duties.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:09.537
Quote, "The canning, processing,
preserving, freezing,

00:07:09.537 --> 00:07:11.887
"drawing, marketing,
storing, packing for shipment

00:07:11.887 --> 00:07:14.247
"or distribution of agricultural produce,

00:07:14.247 --> 00:07:16.600
"meat and fish products,
and perishable foods."

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:17.880
So, what's the problem here?

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:21.487
Well the dispute turns on just
one word or, in the phrase

00:07:21.487 --> 00:07:24.230
"shipment or distribution"
or specifically,

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.850
or in the absence of an Oxford comma.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:27.960
And, why does this matter?

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.057
Well here's how NPR's Colin
Dwyer explained it back in 2017.

00:07:31.057 --> 00:07:33.647
"The presence of that tiny
conjunction without a comma

00:07:33.647 --> 00:07:36.217
"as a companion, makes
for some muddled meanings.

00:07:36.217 --> 00:07:38.267
"Is packing for shipment or distribution

00:07:38.267 --> 00:07:40.007
"exempt from overtime regulations?

00:07:40.007 --> 00:07:43.550
"Or are both packing in shipment
and distribution exempt?"

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:45.730
These aren't idle questions
for the five delivery drivers

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:47.760
who sued Oakhurst,
because as court's notes,

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:49.240
the drivers do distribute

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:51.270
but they do not pack the perishable foods.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.770
In other words, one
interpretation of the laws list,

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:55.860
would make the drivers
eligible for overtime pay.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:58.400
The other would mean, they
won't get those extra dollars

00:07:58.400 --> 00:07:59.960
for extra time on the job.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:02.530
So in this lawsuit, the
delivery drivers argued

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.980
that the quote "packing for
shipment or distribution"

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.270
refers to the single activity of packing,

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:09.260
regardless of whether the
packing is for shipment

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:10.480
or for distribution.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:12.410
And since the drivers
handle perishable food

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:14.450
but do not engage in packing them,

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:17.400
they argue that they are not
exempt workers under the law.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.277
In response, Oakhurst
countered that the phrase

00:08:19.277 --> 00:08:21.320
"packing for shipment or distribution,"

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:23.870
refers to two distinct exempt activities.

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:25.840
The first being the packing for shipment

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:27.560
and the second being distribution.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:28.760
Since the truckers do engage

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:30.380
in the distribution of dairy products,

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:31.970
which are perishable foods,

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:34.420
the truckers are exempt from overtime pay.

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:36.760
A federal court in Maine
agreed with Oakhurst

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:38.300
ruling that under state law,

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:40.600
the drivers were not
entitled to overtime pay.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:42.610
And the court concluded
that the Maine Legislature

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:45.330
intended to describe two
distinct exempt opportunities,

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.320
and there was no dispute
that the truckers do engage

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:49.240
in the distribution of dairy products.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:50.820
But a three judge panel

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:52.970
on the First Circuit
Court of Appeals disagreed

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:54.910
and reversed the lower courts decision.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:57.150
Judge David Barron
opened the 29 page ruling

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.137
saying that quote, "For want of a comma,

00:08:59.137 --> 00:09:00.980
"we have this case."

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:03.120
The court reasoned that
the lack of an Oxford comma

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.200
between shipment and quote,
"or distribution of,"

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:08.360
meant that both the phrases referred back

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.090
to the singular activity packing,

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:12.600
and since the drivers deliver
products but don't pack them,

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:14.660
they were not covered
by the main exemption

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:15.800
for overtime pay.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:17.700
Judge Barron concluded
that the lack of a comma

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:18.900
made the Statute ambiguous,

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.570
and under the rules of
interpreting Statutes,

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:23.070
any ambiguity must be construed liberally.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:24.400
And as a result the court unanimously

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:27.020
sided with the drivers,
sending the case back down

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:27.853
to the lower court.

00:09:27.853 --> 00:09:31.380
And shortly thereafter, the
parties settled for $5 million.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:33.400
Afterwards, the Maine
legislature stepped in

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:34.690
and addressed the punctuation issue.

00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:37.170
But, I think they might
have overcompensated

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:38.320
just a little bit.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.267
Take a look at how the law is written now.

00:09:40.267 --> 00:09:44.147
"The canning; processing; preserving;

00:09:44.147 --> 00:09:45.780
"freezing;

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:48.300
drawing; marketing;

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:49.944
storing;

00:09:49.944 --> 00:09:51.767
"packing for shipment;

00:09:51.767 --> 00:09:53.483
"or distributing of:

00:09:54.437 --> 00:09:56.837
"sub one agricultural produce;

00:09:56.837 --> 00:09:59.057
"sub two meat and fish products;

00:09:59.057 --> 00:10:01.487
"and sub three perishable foods."

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:04.920
And when asked about replacing
all of the punctuation

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.017
with semi-colons, the
Maine legislature said,

00:10:07.017 --> 00:10:07.920
"No comma."

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:09.040
All right, that takes us to the third

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:12.040
And potentially most
important comma in History.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.627
As Sir Roger Casement
said quote, "God deliver

00:10:14.627 --> 00:10:16.387
"from such antiquaries is these

00:10:16.387 --> 00:10:18.307
"to hang a man's life upon a comma

00:10:18.307 --> 00:10:20.300
"and throttle him with a semi-colon."

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:22.490
When a lawyer takes a client's defense,

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:24.500
particularly one punishable by death,

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:27.270
the best strategy is to force
the state to prove their case

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:30.340
and avoid making any damaging
admissions on your end.

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:32.220
But that doesn't always go as planned,

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:33.730
particularly when you have a client

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:35.010
like Sir Roger casement.

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:36.750
Charged with high treason against England

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:37.860
during World War I.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:39.370
Not only did he refuse to deny

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:41.030
the underlying charges against him,

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:43.380
but Sir Roger, an Irish separatists,

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:44.780
wanted to give a passionate speech

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:46.610
explaining why his purported treason,

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:49.480
in the name of Irish independence,
was morally justified,

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:51.290
even during World War I.

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:52.870
Seeking to prevent his client

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:54.800
from making the state's case for him,

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:55.820
Sir Roger's lawyer,

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:59.400
in what might have been a
miscalculation of legal strategy,

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:02.430
rested his client's defense
primarily on punctuation,

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:04.080
namely the interpretation of a comma

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:05.900
in the underlying treason Statute.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:07.360
And prior to his arrest for treason,

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.710
Roger Casement was a
highly respected diplomat,

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:11.070
described as the father

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:13.300
of 20th century human
rights investigations.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:16.880
He became Sir Roger Case when
upon being knighted in 1911,

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:19.240
for detailing Belgian colonial atrocities

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:21.120
and human rights abuses in Peru.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:22.520
And despite his service to the Crown,

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:25.480
Sir Roger was an anti-imperialist
Irish separatist,

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:28.130
who wanted to see Ireland
independent from Britain.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:30.200
And after being knighted,
Sir Roger became active

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:32.240
in Irish Republican Nationalism.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:33.490
In November, 1914,

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:36.110
just four months after the
outbreak of World War I,

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:38.770
Sir Roger negotiated a formal
declaration from Germany,

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:41.537
then at war with Britain,
pledging that quote,

00:11:41.537 --> 00:11:44.520
"under no circumstances would
Germany invade Ireland."

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:47.047
And Germany supported
Ireland's quest for quote,

00:11:47.047 --> 00:11:49.200
"national prosperity
and national freedom."

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.350
Sir Roger personally went to
Germany to seek military aid

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.380
in his quest to gain Irish independence.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:55.810
Sir Roger also sought to persuade

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:57.430
Irish prisoners of war in Germany

00:11:57.430 --> 00:11:59.200
to renounce their allegiance to the King,

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:02.570
and form an Irish brigade to
fight back against the British.

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:05.480
But for collaborating with
Germany during World War I,

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:08.360
Sir Roger casement was arrested
and charged with treason.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:11.140
The Treason Act of 1351,

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:12.930
which had been translated into English

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:14.900
from its original Norman French,

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.137
included the following pertinent text.

00:12:17.137 --> 00:12:19.277
"If a man do levy war against our Lord

00:12:19.277 --> 00:12:22.017
"the King in his Realm, or be
adherent to the Kings Enemies

00:12:22.017 --> 00:12:25.037
"in his Realm, giving to them
Aid and Comfort in the Realm

00:12:25.037 --> 00:12:27.810
"or elsewhere," et cetera, et cetera.

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:29.537
Now, the defense argued
that the phrase quote,

00:12:29.537 --> 00:12:31.767
"or elsewhere" applied only to the words

00:12:31.767 --> 00:12:33.630
"Aid and Comfort in the Realm,"

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:35.727
but had no applications
the words "be adherent

00:12:35.727 --> 00:12:37.480
"to the King's enemies in the Realm,"

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:41.120
or "levy War against our
Lord, the King in his Realm."

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:42.380
According to this interpretation,

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:44.160
the Treaty Act only
criminalized activities

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:45.660
carried out on British soil,

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:47.830
while activities carried
out outside Britain,

00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:50.950
like the ones that Sir
Roger did in Germany,

00:12:50.950 --> 00:12:53.130
did not qualify as treason under the law.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:55.210
The court rejected this interpretation,

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:57.480
treating the commas
around "Aid and Comfort"

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.260
like a parenthetical phrase.

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:01.640
And the court concluded
that treason was treason,

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:03.530
whether committed at home or abroad.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:05.600
And after all of his
appeals were exhausted,

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:09.280
Sir Roger casement was hanged
in London in August, 1916.

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:12.530
And such was the origin of
Sir Roger's famous epigram.

00:13:12.530 --> 00:13:14.190
Because when Sir Roger complained

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:15.920
that he was hanged on a comma,

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:18.550
he was criticizing his
lawyers losing legal strategy,

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:20.260
focusing on punctuation

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:22.750
and not criticizing the statute itself.

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:24.180
In lieu of grammatical analysis,

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:26.160
Sir Roger wanted to give a flowery speech

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:28.457
defending his actions to
essentially tell the court,

00:13:28.457 --> 00:13:30.927
"Yes, I committed treason,
but I would do it again.

00:13:30.927 --> 00:13:32.940
"If it meant that Ireland could be free."

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:34.470
So, there you have it Legal Eagles

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:36.790
Grammar and punctuation
are consequential things.

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:39.860
One misplaced comma can cost
you millions of dollars or,

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:41.440
your life if you're not careful.

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:44.400
So remember, friends don't let
friends draft ambiguous laws.

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:46.760
And if you can, be sure to
get a designated drafter

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:48.920
or you could regret it
for the rest of your life,

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:51.580
or at least until the
next legislative session.

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:53.617
Because otherwise you end
up with questions like,

00:13:53.617 --> 00:13:54.930
"Have you eaten, grandma?"

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:57.820
The difference between being
courteous to your grandmother,

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:00.450
and trying to be a bloodthirsty cannibal.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:02.267
And as it happens, "Have
You Eaten Grandma?:

00:14:02.267 --> 00:14:04.487
"Or, the Life-Saving Importance
of Correct Punctuation,

00:14:04.487 --> 00:14:07.500
"Grammar and Good English" by
Giles Brandreth, is one of the

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:10.050
